TODO.md
NEW_TODO.md
# Frozen copies of the standard files, for diffing a rewrite against its last release.
# Local-only on purpose: tracking them would ship a second copy of AGENTS.md and DESIGN.md to the
# remote, which is the same argument that untracked the notebooks. Backed up by
# tools/backup_local_only.py and re-creatable with tools/snapshot_standards.py --ref <tag>.
docs/standards-history/
# macOS finder metadata
.DS_Store
# Vercel: assembled static output (deploy/vercel/build.sh) + local state
public/
.vercel/
# Netlify local state (deactivated host; see deploy/netlify/)
.netlify/
# Reference material dropped into an exercise's docs/ for an agent to read — third-party or
# unpublished source documents that inform the work but are not ours to redistribute.
**/docs/*.html


# Working handoff notes, wherever they are — a local scratchpad, like TODO.md above.
HANDOFF.md

# Worked solutions kept beside an exercise's code — local reference only, never tracked.
**/src/solution/

# Programme material — the schedule, the class list, and the internal authoring specs. The public
# repo carries the engineering work; the course's own calendar and playbooks stay local.
docs/REQUIREMENTS.md
# Requirement requirement documents, at every level. A requirements document is input for the people and agents building an
# exercise, not public reading, and it is the course's text rather than ours. What we decided and
# why is published instead — each exercise's README.md, and DECISIONS.md where the reasoning is
# long enough to need its own file. Never link to a requirements document from a tracked file: the link resolves
# on a working checkout and 404s for everyone else.
REQUIREMENTS.md
# docs/EXPLAINER_*.md were here. They are TRACKED now: measured against the reference
# material they carry no verbatim runs and no banned vocabulary, because they are
# engineering rules — DOM skeleton, class names, the state-and-render shape — and not
# programme material. Keeping them ignored meant no clone, worktree or CI job could read
# the specification an explainer is graded against.
# Self-critique of a shipped build — useful to us, not part of the published work.
**/docs/DESIGN_CRITIQUE.md

# Topic notebooks are local-only. They are source material rather than this repo's
# deliverable, and executing one bakes real corpus text into a tracked file. `notebooks/hello.ipynb`
# is deliberately NOT matched: it is the tracked sample that keeps the notebook harness under test
# on a fresh clone.
notebooks/S[0-9][0-9]-*.ipynb

# ...with exactly one exception, and it is a requirement rather than a preference. Exercise 10's
# submission says the notebook "should also be there" and offers no alternative — where exercise
# 09's offered "the ipynb file OR training logs", which is why 09's stays local. There is nothing
# to fall back to here.
#
# The rule above exists to keep the course's own material off a public remote. This notebook is our
# training loop: it imports the exercise's package and re-implements nothing, so it carries no more
# of that material than the package does. See 10-training-loop/DECISIONS.md D1.
#
# The negation WORKS because the pattern above is a FILE pattern, not a directory one.
!notebooks/S10-training-loop.ipynb

# ...and so is the script that generates one. A generator is the notebook in another form -- keeping
# it would version the same course material as Python, which is the thing the rule above exists to
# prevent. Both stay on a working checkout and neither is pushed.
#
# The cost is real and is named here rather than discovered later: the builder was the recoverable
# copy, so a lost working tree now loses the notebook AND the means to rebuild it, and CI can no
# longer check that a builder exists or still runs. Back these up outside the repo.
src/exercises/*/tools/build_notebook.py

# ...but the submission bundle IS the deliverable, and the requirements names `run.log` specifically.
#
# This negation WORKS because `*.log` (line 59) is a FILE pattern. The same trick under
# `artifacts/` would be inert: `**/artifacts/` is a DIRECTORY pattern, and git cannot re-include a
# file whose parent directory is excluded -- `git add -A` would report success and stage nothing.
# `tests/test_submission_bundle.py` asserts both halves of that.
!src/exercises/*/submission_artifacts/run.log

# PK's merge order for the open pull requests — local reference, never published.
docs/MERGE_ORDER.md
